## Cred SDK

Implementer:
: Kieran Sweeney (author of this document)

Location:
: https://github.com/cred-ninja/sdk, branch `main`, described here as of commit 87eabb7 (2026-10-20)

License:
: Apache-2.0

Language:
: TypeScript (Node.js)

Maturity:
: Experimental. The implementation follows the architecture of this document (a Delegation Server holding deposited credentials, sub-delegation with attenuation, exercise by proxy) but not yet its token, request, or proof-of-possession formats. It is not wire-compatible with this document.

Contact:
: kieran@kierans.net

Implemented:

- Agent identity ({{agent-identity}}): Ed25519 key pairs and `did:key` identifiers. P-256 is not implemented.
- Protocol version negotiation ({{protocol-version-negotiation}}): the `Cred-Protocol-Version` header, version `0.1.0`, and HTTP 426 with `protocol_version_unsupported`.
- Credential wrapping ({{exercise-flow}}): provider tokens stay in the server's vault and the server makes the upstream call on the agent's behalf. A sub-delegated agent always receives a brokered handle, never a raw provider token.
- Sub-delegation with attenuation ({{chain-integrity}}): wildcard-aware scope containment, a per-permission depth limit, matching service, user, and application context across hops, and a child expiry bounded by the parent's.
- Constraint ceilings ({{constraint-ceilings}}): all eight types, fail-closed parsing of closed constraint objects, uniqueness per (`key`, type), subsumption per hop with no inference across types, and inherit-on-omit. A label-valued `rank` is read with its `order` from the receipt, and a child's `order` must equal its parent's; a label carried without `order` still resolves through a built-in per-key ordering, for receipts minted before `order` was supported. The server holds no running total for `max_lifetime` or `max_subtree`, so it denies any exercise under a cumulative constraint rather than leave it unenforced.
- Chain-verifiable profile ({{chain-verifiable-profile}}): not implemented. The server neither issues nor reads tokens in the {{ASOR}} format; the conformance runner below maps them onto the SDK's hop model.
- Signed receipts: the server mints a compact Ed25519 JWS at each hop carrying `client_id` (the agent DID), a commitment to the parent (SHA-256 over the full compact parent receipt), and a signed lineage of ancestor DIDs. Offline chain verification checks signatures, linkage, depth, scope and ceiling subsumption, and expiry ordering. Receipt payloads are decoded as strict JSON: duplicate member names and integers outside the exact range of an IEEE 754 binary64 are rejected.
- Revocation ({{revocation}}): per-agent and per-connection revocation endpoints. Before minting a child, the server checks the revocation status of every ancestor named in the parent's signed lineage.
- Audit: an audit log of delegation, exercise, and revocation events.

Not implemented, or implemented differently:

- Token exchange: delegation and sub-delegation use custom endpoints, not {{RFC8693}} token exchange.
- Proof of possession: no DPoP. Agents prove key possession with signed payloads and HTTP Message Signatures {{RFC9421}}.
- Capabilities: scope strings plus `constraints`, not {{RFC9396}} `authorization_details`.
- Agent authentication: opaque bearer tokens plus signed payloads, not an {{RFC7523}} assertion.
- Consent: a consent URL the user opens and the agent polls, not CIBA {{OIDC-CIBA}}.
- Token claims: receipts name the agent in both `sub` and `client_id`; the delegating principal is not yet in `sub`, and there is no `act` or `prf` claim. Moving the principal into `sub` is planned for the next wire version.
- Revocation interface and timing: no `DELETE /delegation/{jti}` endpoint or `revoked_count`, and the 1-second and 5-second bounds in {{revocation}} are not measured by tests.

Conformance results. The SDK's chain validation and sub-delegation checks were run against the twenty interop vectors published with draft-asor-wimse-agent-delegation-chain-01 (attenu-guard v0.9.0), against four scope-class vectors proposed to that draft's author, and against the thirty-five vectors published with {{ASOR}} (attenu-guard v0.20.0). At commit 09f9488, all twenty and all four passed; four of the twenty pass with a rejection reason that the runner maps to the vector's reason name. At commit 87eabb7, the twenty still pass, and twenty of the thirty-five pass, five fail, and ten are gaps, in which the property the vector isolates is not represented in the SDK's model, so the SDK's acceptance tests nothing: audience (four vectors), `cnf` (two), the closed and single `agent_delegation` detail (three), and `del_max_depth` per hop (one). The five failures are a valid chain carrying an unrecognized constraint type unchanged from its parent, which the SDK rejects, and four rejections reported under a different reason than the vector's, which the runner does not map: three malformed inputs rejected as not narrower, and a root without a principal rejected as a principal mismatch. The runner, vectors, and pinned results are at https://github.com/cred-ninja/protocol/tree/main/conformance/asor-delegation-chain. These vectors are chains of HS256 tokens in the {{ASOR}} format; the runner checks their signatures and parent hashes itself and maps their claims onto the SDK's hop model. They exercise the scope grammar, constraint subsumption, depth, linkage, expiry ordering, and strict JSON decoding that this document shares with {{ASOR}}. They do not test any token, receipt, or request format defined in this document. The thirty-five-vector set adds `client_id`, `cnf`, principal invariance, the cumulative constraint types, and closed detail objects; its failures and gaps are listed per vector with the pinned results.

# Security Considerations

## Confused Deputy Mitigation

Capability-shaped tokens eliminate ambient authority. An adversarially-prompted agent cannot widen its own capabilities since attenuation is DS-enforced. OAuth access tokens are ambient authority: any code holding the token exercises its full scope. For adversarially-promptable agents, this is a structural exploit vector. Cred delegation tokens are operation-bound, resource-specific, and constraint-bearing, following the principle that designation should equal authority {{CONFUSED-DEPUTY}}.

## Delegation Chain Splicing {#splicing}

The mandatory `prf` chain with DS-signed receipts addresses the vulnerability in Sections 2.1-2.2 of {{RFC8693}} disclosed to the OAuth WG on February 26, 2026 {{OAUTH-SPLICING}}. Each receipt cross-references parent and child DT `jti` values along with both agent DIDs, preventing presentation of tokens from mismatched delegation contexts. The `requested_actor`/`actor_token` binding proposed in {{OBO-USER}} mitigates the same class of attack at the authorization server for the first delegation hop; the `prf` chain extends equivalent protection across every subsequent hop (see {{relationship-to-existing-work}}). The two can compose: a DS can accept an actor-bound access token issued under {{OBO-USER}} as the credential it wraps, then produce `prf` receipts for every sub-delegation beyond that first hop.

## DPoP Binding

DPoP {{RFC9449}} prevents Delegation Token theft and replay by requiring cryptographic proof of private key possession on every `/exercise` request. The proof is bound to the `htm` (HTTP method) and `htu` (URI) of the specific request, preventing re-use across operations.

## Prompt Injection Containment

Because credentials never reach the agent host, a successful prompt injection attack cannot exfiltrate credentials. The agent can only exercise capabilities already granted in its DT, and only via the DS `/exercise` endpoint. The blast radius of a compromised agent is bounded by its current DT's `authorization_details`.

## Chain Depth Limits

Implementations SHOULD enforce a maximum delegation chain depth of 5. Unbounded sub-delegation creates exponential revocation cascades and audit complexity.

## Token Lifetime

The default DT lifetime of 15 minutes limits the blast radius of any single token compromise. Implementations MUST NOT issue DTs with a lifetime exceeding 1 hour.

## Model Identity and Substitution

The `agent_model` and `agent_checksum` claims are self-asserted. A credential valid for an agent remains valid if the operator substitutes the underlying model, a threat class raised against the AIMS predecessor framework in March 2026. Verifying model identity requires attestation of the agent runtime and is out of scope for this document. Deployments that need it should treat these claims as advisory policy input, not proof, and look to remote attestation work in the RATS WG.

## Long-Term Evidence {#long-term-evidence}

Delegation receipts are signed by the DS, with Ed25519 or P-256 unless a deployment uses ML-DSA; a deployment may sign its consent and audit records the same way. A signed record retained as dispute evidence beyond the period in which its signature can be trusted against a cryptographically relevant quantum computer loses its value as evidence unless it was anchored while still trustworthy, for example by periodic time-stamping under a post-quantum signature or by inclusion in an append-only log whose head is so signed. Deployments that retain signed records for that long SHOULD anchor them. Consent and audit records that are immutable but unsigned carry evidence only as strong as the integrity controls of their store; a deployment that relies on them as dispute evidence SHOULD commit their digests to such a log.

# IANA Considerations

This document requests registration of:

- `cred_delegation` as an `authorization_details` type in the OAuth Authorization Server Metadata registry (per {{RFC9396}})
- `agent_model`, `agent_operator`, `consent_id`, `credential_handle`, `prf` as JWT claim names in the JSON Web Token Claims registry
- `urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:cred-delegation` as a URI in the OAuth Parameters registry

